content.text:
Confirmed — the generalized `classifyUpdateConflict` (already in `service.go` from the PATCH/impression work) doesn't care how many checks are passed or what they check:

```go
func (s *Service) classifyUpdateConflict(ctx context.Context, id int64, checks ...conflictCheck) error {
    current, err := s.repo.GetByID(ctx, id)
    if errors.Is(err, pgx.ErrNoRows) {
        return httpx.NotFound("campaign not found")
    }
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    for _, check := range checks {
        if check.failed(current) {
            return httpx.Conflict(check.message)
        }
    }
    return httpx.Conflict("campaign was modified concurrently, please retry")
}
```

For `resume`'s three checks (`not paused`, `no budget left`, `end date passed`), the loop runs all three against the re-fetched row; if none of them evaluate `true`, it falls through the loop unconditionally to that last line — no change needed to the function itself, this is exactly why it was generalized to take a variadic list rather than a fixed set of conditions.

Concretely, this fallback path only gets reached for `resume` when: `repo.Resume`'s atomic `UPDATE` found 0 matching rows at that instant (so the row was *not* resumable then), but by the time `classifyUpdateConflict`'s re-read runs a moment later, the row looks resumable again (paused, budget available, end date still in the future). That can only happen if some other concurrent write changed the row back into a resumable state in between — a genuine race, correctly reported as "modified concurrently, please retry" rather than a made-up specific reason, since none of the three real reasons actually apply at classification time.

